# Host-side build tools and libraries that mise can't pin (none are in its
# registry) and that a stock macOS or a minimal Linux box doesn't have.
# Install with:
#
#   brew bundle --file=plugins/netsurf/Brewfile
#
# On Debian/Ubuntu the equivalent is:
#   apt install libpng-dev pkg-config flex bison cmake
#
# Everything here runs on or links into THIS machine, not the wasm target:
#   libpng + pkg-config  netsurf's own convert_image build tool links real
#                        libpng; build.sh reads its flags via pkg-config
#                        BEFORE PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR is repointed at the wasm
#                        sysroot.
#   flex + bison         libnslog's filter grammar is generated at build time
#                        (the outputs are plain C, then cross-compiled), and
#                        nsgenbind needs bison >= 2.4 — newer than the 2.3
#                        stock macOS ships, which is why build-deps.sh carries
#                        the compat/nslog-bison23.h fallback.
#   gperf                libhubbub generates its element-type perfect hash
#                        with it (macOS ships one; Debian does not).
#   m4                   bison shells out to it and fails with a bare
#                        "m4 subprocess failed"; Debian has no /usr/bin/m4.
#   cmake                build-deps.sh cross-builds libpng for wasi with it.
brew "libpng"
# pkg-config: needed on macOS, which ships none. On LINUX, skip this one and use
# the distro's — brew's pkgconf takes over `pkg-config` on PATH and then answers
# for system libraries too, resolving them to brew builds made against a newer
# glibc. That breaks native links far from here (wk-server's tests stop linking
# against libasound). build.sh adds brew's .pc dir to PKG_CONFIG_PATH itself, so
# the system pkg-config still finds the libpng above.
brew "pkg-config"
brew "flex"
brew "bison"
brew "gperf"
brew "m4"
brew "cmake"
